{{also|conjuré}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|conjurer|}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|coniūrō||I swear together; conspire}}, from {{m|la|con-||with, together}} + {{m|la|iūro||I swear or take an oath}}.

===Pronunciation===
* Senses relating to magic tricks and imagination:
** {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈkʌnd͡ʒə(ɹ)/|/ˈkɒnd͡ʒə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
** {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈkɑnd͡ʒəɹ/|lang=en}}<!-- generated by [[Template:X2IPA]] -->
** {{rhymes|ʌndʒə(ɹ)|ɒndʒə(ɹ)|lang=en}}
* Senses relating to religious or solemn actions:
** {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/kənˈd͡ʒʊə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|conjur}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[perform]] [[magic trick]]s.
#: ''He started '''conjuring''' at the age of 15, and is now a famous stage magician.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[summon]] (a [[devil]], etc.) using [[supernatural]] [[power]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To [[practice]] [[black magic]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[enchant]] or [[bewitch]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[evoke]]. {{rfex|lang=en}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[imagine]] or [[picture]] in the [[mind]].
#: {{synonyms|en|envisage|imagine|picture|visualize}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To make an [[urgent]] [[request]] to; to [[appeal]] to or [[beseech]].
#* Addison
#*: I '''conjure''' you, let him know, / Whate'er was done against him, Cato did it.
#* '''1851''', Herman Melville, ''Moby-Dick'':
#*: Stammering out something, I knew not what, I rolled away from him against the wall, and then '''conjured''' him, whoever or whatever he might be, to keep quiet, and let me get up and light the lamp again.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[conspire]] or [[plot]]. <!--a Latinism-->
#* Milton
#*: Drew after him the third part of Heaven's sons / '''Conjured''' against the Highest.

